  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(3,2)$ and passes through the point $(21,-4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(3,2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n{(y-2)}^{2}=4p(x-3).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(21,-4),$ we substitute $x=21$ and $y=-4$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-4-2)}^{2} & =4p(21-3) \\\\ {(-6)}^{2} & =4p\\cdot (18) \\\\ 36 & =72p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =2(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-2)^{2} & =2(x-3) \\\\ 4 & =2(x-3) \\\\ 2 & =x-3 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $5.$",
